  <title>6.3.7: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01611</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.3.7: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2fca98b7-aa39-4acc-9f9b-849330256337</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>To help students summarize the skills they learned dividing polynomials, emphasize the following points:</para>
<list>
  <item> Divide monomials by monomials by dividing the coefficients and then dividing the variables using the Quotient Property for exponents. </item>
  <item> Divide a polynomial by a monomial by distributing the division of the monomial to each term in the polynomial dividend. </item>
  <item> The solution to your division problem can alway be checked by multiplying the solution by the divisor to see if the product matches the original dividend. </item>
  <item> To divide a polynomial by another polynomial, use long division as you have previously learned using numbers. </item>
  <item> An efficient method to divide a polynomial by a binomial is synthetic division. </item>
  <list>
    <item> The divisor must be in the form \(x - c\), where \(c\) is a constant. </item>
    <item> Use a placeholder with a \(0\) coefficient, such as \(0x^2\), for any term that is missing in the dividend from the term with the highest degree down to a degree of \(0\). </item>
    <item> The bottom-right number in the third row of the synthetic division is the remainder. </item>
  </list>
  <item> Polynomial functions can be divided using the same methods as with other polynomials. </item>
  <item> Use the Remainder Theorem to easily find the value of a remainder in any polynomial division problem. </item>
  <item> Use the Factor Theorem to determine if a divisor is a factor of a larger polynomial. </item>
</list></section>
</content>
